\chapter{Testing}\label{Testing}

The tests live in the same files as the code they verify, right after it,
and [[make test]] tangles them into [[tests/test_*.py]] and runs [[pytest]].
This chapter covers what the individual tests share: how a test gets a LADOK
session, how the tests that talk to LADOK can run without it, and how we keep
personal data out of what we record.

\section{Three kinds of tests}

The tests fall into three groups, by what they need from LADOK.
\begin{description}
\item[Offline tests] exercise our own code: parsing, the command-line
  interface, the reporting logic, session sharing.
  They use real objects where they can and small local stand-ins where a
  LADOK response is needed.
  They need no network and no credentials.
\item[Read tests] send requests to LADOK and check the responses.
  Most of them are in the chapters on the API calls.
\item[Write tests] create, update, finalize and remove results.
  They are marked [[mutates]].
\end{description}

LADOK's test environment is not a sandbox filled with made-up data.
It is a copy of production, synced again every week, so the students in it
are real people and a write changes a real record until the next sync.
Logging in also takes multi-factor authentication, which needs a person at
hand.
Yet we want [[make test]] to check the code that talks to LADOK, offline and
without anyone at hand.

We therefore record LADOK's responses once and replay them.
The read and write tests run in three modes, with the same test code:
\begin{description}
\item[Replay] is the default: [[make test]].
  Each test that talks to LADOK gets its responses from a recording, a
  \emph{cassette} in [[tests/cassettes]].
  No request leaves the machine, and no credentials are needed.
  A test without a cassette is skipped, with the reason.
\item[Live] is [[make test PYTESTFLAGS=--live]].
  Every test runs against LADOK's test environment, and the responses are
  recorded afresh, but into a temporary directory, never over the cassettes
  in [[tests/cassettes]].
  Afterwards we compare the structure of the fresh recordings with the
  ones we replay (\cref{Drift}).
  This tells us when LADOK has changed under us, which a replay never can.
\item[Recording] is [[make test PYTESTFLAGS=--record-mode=once]], which
  records the cassettes that are missing, or [[--record-mode=rewrite]], which
  records them all again.
  It talks to LADOK like a live run but writes to [[tests/cassettes]].
\end{description}
Write tests change data, so they reach LADOK only when we also give
[[--live-writes]]; in replay they replay like the others.

The cassettes are not in the repository.
Even pseudonymized, they hold the records of whole groups of real students
(the participants of a course round, their grades and dates), linked by
LADOK's UIDs, and whoever holds the key can undo the pseudonyms.
They therefore stay on the machine that recorded them, and [[tests/.gitignore]]
keeps them out of git.
Anyone who can log in to LADOK's test environment can record their own
(\cref{RecordingOwn}); without cassettes, the tests that talk to LADOK are
skipped, and the offline tests still run.
Both live runs and recording need the key that pseudonymizes the recordings
(\cref{Pseudonymization}).

Earlier, the test modules created their session at import time.
That had two bad effects.
Without credentials, the import failed, so [[pytest]] could not collect the
module, and its offline tests never ran either.
With credentials, a plain [[make test]] was a live run: it logged in, could
prompt for MFA and wrote results, and [[make publish]] runs [[make test]]
before every release.
Taking the session from a fixture fixes both problems.
A fixture runs only when a test asks for it, so it can skip that test or hand
it an offline session instead of failing the import.

\section{The shared test configuration}

[[pytest]] reads command-line options and shared fixtures from a file
[[conftest.py]] in the test directory, which we tangle from here.
The recording itself is done by the [[pytest-recording]] plugin, which wraps
[[vcrpy]].
A test marked [[@pytest.mark.vcr]] gets a cassette named after the test,
and [[--record-mode]] chooses between replaying and recording.
We add the live mode, the LADOK session, and the privacy protection.

The scrubbing and the drift comparison come from [[vcr_support.py]].
It is not ours: it is a helper from the [[testing]] skill for Claude Code,
committed unchanged in [[tests/]] so that it can be updated from its source
(the copy is from commit d2de183 of the skills repository).
Everything that is specific to LADOK we pass to it from here.
<<[[conftest.py]]>>=
