By default [[vcrpy]] matches a request to a recorded one by its method and
URL.
That is enough for reading, but not for writing: a write test that sent the
wrong grade would get the recorded response, with the right grade, and pass.
The configuration from [[vcr_support]] therefore matches the body too, so
that in replay a write must send exactly what was recorded.


\section{Checks after the run}\label{Drift}

After a run that recorded anything, we scan what was written for personal
data and secrets: every original value that the pseudonymizer replaced, and
anything shaped like an email address, a personnummer or a token.
A hit fails the run and is reported as a file, a line and a redacted stub,
never the value.

After a live run we also compare the fresh recordings with the ones we
replay.
The comparison looks at structure only: status codes, content types, and the
paths and types of the keys in the JSON bodies, never the values.
A removed key, a changed type or a request that is no longer made is
\emph{drift}: the cassettes no longer describe LADOK, and the tests that
replay them may pass on behaviour that LADOK has dropped.
Drift fails the run.
The fix is to adapt the code if needed and then record the affected tests
again with [[--record-mode=rewrite -k]] \emph{test}, never to edit a
cassette by hand.
<<checks after the run>>=
def pytest_sessionfinish(session, exitstatus):
  config = session.config
  pairs = getattr(config, "_recorded_dirs", set())
  if not pairs:
    return
  reporter = config.pluginmanager.get_plugin("terminalreporter")

  def write(message, **markup):
    if reporter:
      reporter.write_line(message, **markup)
    else:
      print(message)

  <<scan the recordings for personal data>>
  if config.getoption("--live"):
    <<compare the live recordings with the cassettes>>
