\section{Recording your own cassettes}\label{RecordingOwn}

To record, we need a login to LADOK's test environment, through
[[ladok login]], and a pseudonymization key.
Any long random string will do as a key, but it must stay the same between
recordings and it must never be written to the repository or printed.
With GNOME Keyring we can create one without ever seeing it:
\begin{minted}{bash}
python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32), end='')" \
  | secret-tool store --label='ladok3 test pseudonymization key' \
      service ladok3 key test-pseudonym
\end{minted}
Then we record the cassettes that are missing, writes included, reading the
key from the keyring for this command only:
\begin{minted}{bash}
TEST_PSEUDONYM_KEY="$(secret-tool lookup service ladok3 key test-pseudonym)" \
  make test PYTESTFLAGS="--record-mode=once --live-writes"
\end{minted}
A live run takes the key the same way, with [[PYTESTFLAGS=--live]].
The recording fails if the scan afterwards finds personal data
(\cref{Drift}); then extend the key lists in \cref{Pseudonymization}, delete
the cassettes and record again.

The tests use records in KTH's part of the test environment: a test student,
the courses DD2395 and DD1301, their rounds and LAB1 modules,
KTH's organisational unit and a result reporter.
Their UIDs are constants in the tests of the API calls.
At another institution, those constants must be replaced by records there,
and the write tests need a login with the right to report results on the
module they write to.

